/*! \internal
    \page qtros2-code-generation Qt ROS 2 Code Generation Pipeline
    \tableofcontents

    Every Qt ROS 2 interface module (\c qtros2_std_msgs, \c qtros2_geometry_msgs,
    \c qtros2_sensor_msgs, ...) is mostly generated code. This page documents what
    feeds the generator, what comes out, and how the pieces fit together.

    \section qtros2-codegen-why Why generate at all

    The headers in \c /opt/ros/.../include — produced by \c rosidl_generator_cpp —
    expose ROS-native C++ types (\c std::string, \c std::vector, allocator-templated
    structs). Those don't satisfy the requirements of a Qt module:

    - QML needs \c Q_GADGET value types with \c Q_PROPERTY fields, not bare structs.
    - Publishers/subscribers/service-clients/action-clients need to be \c QObject
      subclasses with signals so they integrate with the Qt event loop and QML
      bindings.
    - Qt has its own container vocabulary (\c QString, \c QList, \c QByteArray,
      \c QStringList) that's more ergonomic than \c std::* on the QML side.
    - We need conversion shims (\c toRos / \c fromRos) between the two type systems
      at the publisher/subscriber boundary.

    No stock OMG IDL compiler or rosidl generator emits that shape, so we ship our
    own \c rosidl_generator_* plugin. It parses the same IDL the rest of ROS 2 uses
    and emits Qt-flavored wrappers around the \c rosidl_generator_cpp types.

    \section qtros2-codegen-inputs Inputs: .msg, .srv, .action — but really .idl

    Authors write \c .msg, \c .srv, or \c .action files in the historical ROS
    format. Upstream \c rosidl_adapter converts each one to an equivalent \c .idl
    (OMG IDL) during the interface package's build. Pre-built packages in
    \c /opt/ros/jazzy ship both side-by-side (e.g.
    \c std_msgs/msg/ColorRGBA.msg and \c std_msgs/msg/ColorRGBA.idl); the \c .idl
    header literally says <em>"generated from rosidl_adapter ... with input from
    std_msgs/msg/ColorRGBA.msg"</em>.

    \c rosidl_generator_qtros2 reads only the \c .idl. All generators in the ROS 2
    ecosystem do — \c rosidl_parser.parse_idl_file() is the single canonical entry
    point.

    \section qtros2-codegen-pipeline The pipeline

    \startuml
    !include code-generation-pipeline.puml
    \enduml

    ① Upstream \c rosidl_adapter converts \c .msg / \c .srv / \c .action to \c .idl
    (or the package ships pre-adapted \c .idl, as the ROS 2 core interfaces do).

    ② \c rosidl_parser parses each \c .idl into an in-memory IR — \c Message,
    \c Service, \c Action objects from \c rosidl_parser.definition.

    ③ The user (or the Qt build system for the built-in modules) calls
    \c qtros2_generate_from_package(SOURCE_PACKAGE pkg TARGET Foo ...).
    This macro is <b>not</b> registered in the rosidl plugin registry — it is
    explicitly invoked and is the only entry point to the generator.
    The macro collects the source package's IDL tuples and dependency lists,
    sets the same \c rosidl_generate_interfaces_* variables that
    \c rosidl_generate_interfaces() would set, then does
    \c include(rosidl_generator_qtros2_generate_interfaces.cmake) directly.

    ④ \c rosidl_generator_qtros2_generate_interfaces.cmake writes a JSON
    arguments file (IDL tuples, output dir, template dir, dependency packages)
    and invokes the generator at <b>CMake configure time</b> via
    \c execute_process — early enough that IDEs and \c qmllint see the files.

    ⑤ \c python3 -m \c rosidl_generator_qtros2 calls
    \c generate_qtros2() in \c rosidl_generator_qtros2/__init__.py. For each
    \c idl_tuple it parses the IDL, walks messages/services/actions, builds a
    context dict (the message IR plus helper callables like \c msg_type_to_qt,
    \c get_qt_class_name, \c needs_wrapper_type), and runs an \c em.Interpreter
    over each EmPy template in \c resource/\c *.em.

    ⑥ The generator writes a manifest of every file it produced —
    \c qtros2_generated_files.cmake — listing headers and sources for CMake to
    pick up.

    ⑦ The CMake hook then calls \c generate_cmake_vars() in the same Python
    module to emit \c qtros2_module_vars.cmake, which packages everything
    \c qt_add_qml_module() needs (\c SOURCES, \c IMPORTS, \c PUBLIC_INCLUDE_DIRECTORIES,
    \c PUBLIC_LIBRARIES, \c LIBRARIES). The hook \c include()s that file and calls
    \c qt_internal_add_qml_module() (in-tree Qt build) or \c qt_add_library +
    \c qt_add_qml_module() (external app build).

    \section qtros2-codegen-artifacts Per-IDL artifacts

    \startuml
    !include code-generation-artifacts.puml
    \enduml

    For every \c msg/Foo.idl with ≥ 2 fields, four \c .{hpp,cpp} pairs are
    emitted (from \c msg__value_type, \c msg__publisher, \c msg__subscriber
    templates):

    \li \c foo.hpp / \c foo.cpp — \c Q_GADGET value type. \c Q_PROPERTY for each
        field; \c toRos() / \c fromRos() convert to/from the
        \c rosidl_generator_cpp type. Registered as a QML value type with the
        name derived by \c get_qml_value_type_name().
    \li \c foo_publisher.hpp / \c foo_publisher.cpp — \c QObject subclass with
        a \c publish() method.
    \li \c foo_subscriber.hpp / \c foo_subscriber.cpp — \c QObject subclass
        emitting a \c received() signal whenever a message arrives on the
        subscribed topic.

    Single-field and empty messages skip the wrapper value type
    (\c needs_wrapper_type() returns false): the inner type appears directly
    in the publisher/subscriber API.

    For \c srv/Foo.idl, \c srv__client templates emit a \c FooServiceClient
    \c QObject. The request and response messages are then run back through
    \c _generate_message_artifacts() to produce \c Foo_Request and
    \c Foo_Response value types (no publisher/subscriber pair — they're not
    independently transportable).

    For \c action/Foo.idl, \c action__client templates emit a
    \c FooActionClient \c QObject; goal, result, and feedback are emitted as
    value types via the same shared helper.

    \section qtros2-codegen-naming Naming and namespaces

    Three name transformations matter:

    \li <b>Qt namespace</b> — \c get_qt_namespace(package_name) strips a leading
        \c qtros2_ and a trailing \c _msgs, then CamelCases the remainder and
        prepends \c Qtros2 and appends \c Msgs. So \c geometry_msgs becomes
        \c Qtros2GeometryMsgs. The C++ namespace.
    \li <b>Qt class name</b> — \c get_qt_class_name(package, message_name)
        strips underscores from the message name. \c BatteryState stays
        \c BatteryState; \c Time_Source becomes \c TimeSource. The QML value
        type uses the same name (no package prefix); the QML URI
        (\c QtRos2.GeometryMsgs, etc.) provides scoping.
    \li <b>File naming</b> — \c to_snake_case() converts the message name to
        \c snake_case for filenames, matching \c rosidl_generator_cpp's
        conventions so the same string can be used to \c #include the upstream
        header.

    Type mapping happens in \c MSG_TYPE_TO_QT / \c MSG_TYPE_TO_CPP plus
    \c msg_type_to_qt_full(), which lifts \c Array / \c BoundedSequence /
    \c UnboundedSequence into \c QList, \c QByteArray, or \c QStringList per
    inner type.

    \section qtros2-codegen-build-time When generation runs

    Generation runs at <b>configure time</b>, not build time. The hook compares
    \c mtime on every IDL file and on every template + Python source against
    a stamp file (\c .${target}_qtros2_last_gen); if all are older, it skips
    the Python invocation. Building from a clean tree always generates; editing
    a template or an \c .idl triggers a single regeneration on the next CMake
    reconfigure.

    Why configure-time and not a \c add_custom_command at build time? Qt
    Creator, \c qmllint, and other tooling read the source list during CMake
    configuration. If the generated files don't exist at that point, headers
    show up as missing in the IDE even though \c make would later succeed.

    \section qtros2-codegen-empy A note on EmPy

    The rest of the \c rosidl_generator_* ecosystem
    (\c rosidl_generator_cpp, \c rosidl_generator_c, \c rosidl_generator_py)
    uses EmPy templates; we follow suit for consistency, not because it's the
    template engine we'd pick today. \c rosidl_pycommon provides a unified
    helper for expanding templates with EmPy 3 / EmPy 4 compatibility shims,
    but this generator currently drives \c em.Interpreter directly (see
    \c _generate_message_artifacts() in \c __init__.py). If upstream ever
    migrates off EmPy, the natural replacement would be Jinja2 — but no such
    migration is scheduled at time of writing.
*/
